<mxCell id="s3-edge" value="Large payloads" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;strokeWidth=2;strokeColor=#c62828;dashed=1;endArrow=classic;endFill=1;fontSize=10" edge="1" parent="1" source="boundary" target="s3"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="analogy" value="BEGINNER ANALOGY: The platform is a restaurant kitchen with a ticket rail system. Users (customers) submit orders (jobs) via the API (cashier). The Scheduler (head chef) reads the reservation book (Cassandra) every 30 seconds, finds meals due in the next 60 seconds, and hangs tickets on the rail (Redis ZPOPMIN queue) sorted by cook-by time. Line cooks (Workers) grab tickets atomically (only one cook per ticket), prepare the meal (execute job), and ring a bell every 30 seconds (heartbeat) to confirm they're still cooking. If a cook disappears mid-meal without ringing the bell for 90 seconds, the supervisor (Monitor Service) notices the silent station, marks that ticket failed, and hangs a duplicate on the rail for another cook. The head chef (Scheduler) is elected by seniority (Redis SETNX leader election) — only one chef decides what tickets go up to avoid duplicate orders. Midnight rush (thundering herd) is smoothed by randomly staggering ticket times by 0-5 minutes (jitter). The kitchen notebook (Cassandra) records every order and outcome for auditing." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e1f5fe;strokeColor=#0277bd;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="50" y="840" width="1530" height="105" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
